# ClaimGuard Phase 1 — Production Deployment Runbook (Revised)
## Persistent File-Backed H2 Database + Spring Security Authentication & RBAC

> **Document Reference:** `CLAIMGUARD_PHASE1_DEPLOYMENT_RUNBOOK_REVISED.md`  
> **Date:** October 25, 2026  
> **Target Container:** `claimguard-staging` (Host Port: `8083`, Container Port: `8080`)  
> **Verified Image Under Deployment:** `claimguard:phase1-candidate` (`sha256:2fd25b07fba5787db431f3b2f10edaf8594d2fb5536580b92029ef7d58497a97`)  
> **Verified Preserved Container:** `claimguard-staging` (`910771a20872`) -> `claimguard-staging-prephase1`  
> **Production Persistent Volume:** `claimguard-data:/app/data`  
> **Operational Status:** PREPARATORY ONLY — No deployment commands have been executed.  

---

## 1. Architectural & Operational Summary

This runbook defines the exact, step-by-step procedure to transition the ClaimGuard Staging service from its legacy in-memory configuration (`claimguard:stitch-ui-staging`) to the Phase 1 persistent, authenticated architecture (`claimguard:phase1-candidate`).

### Core Safety Rules
1. **Never Remove Containers:** The original container is renamed to `claimguard-staging-prephase1` and kept in a stopped state. It is never deleted.
2. **Never Remove Volumes:** The named volume `claimguard-data` is preserved through any operational contingency, including rollbacks.
3. **Data Loss Preemption:** Because the active container uses volatile `jdbc:h2:mem:claimguard`, stopping it clears RAM. The runbook executes an exhaustive, read-only REST API export of all claims and review history prior to initiating any state change.
4. **Credential Isolation:** Passwords are captured interactively as secure strings and passed to the container via a temporary, single-use environment file with immediate deletion, eliminating plain-text exposure in shell histories and process command lines.

